hi there  steve here um i'm having trouble with my  uh whole  video production system here uh for some  reason uh youtube is rejecting my  imovie movies so i'm gonna do a short  one here and just see if it works i've  made a few changes  i'm going to talk about philosophy for  children i was listening to a  a podcast on tsf podcast out of portugal  i'm going to put the link and notes to  this video and there was an interview  with a gentleman who was proposing  you know uh to uh have more philosophy  for kids  from the ages of i guess six seven or so  up to nine 10 11 in schools  and i thought was very interesting  because he said that number one  that the first thing is to teach kids to  listen  and i think it's very important for  people to learn to listen  just as well as it is to learn to read  and we know that a lot of people who  have trouble  reading they have trouble because they  haven't heard very much they haven't  listened to  a lot of stuff at home maybe their  parents didn't talk about  things in whatever language the native  language  um so the more people listen the more  kids listen the more they're aware of  different things  uh have general knowledge the easier it  is for them to read the  easier it is for them to to infer and  understand things they don't need  teachers to teach them critical thinking  they need to have heard a lot so  listening number one  yes philosophy for children teach kids  to listen  number two is that the group the class  20 people or so becomes a kind of a  community of  inquiry of investigation they are going  to collectively  study a subject debated discuss it  whatever but it's a community  that is discussing things excellent  third point is that they're going to  debate  but they're not going to arrive at a  consensus this is not about  a consensus and this is very much in  contrast to the prevailing  ideology in north american academia  where  you have to kind of dialogue and see if  we can't find  common ground and we have to agree no  no disagree learn to disagree  learn to debate learn to defend your  ideas and learn to accept  that other people have different ideas  and that's perfectly  acceptable everyone doesn't have to  agree because what happens is  when you have this this ideology of  everyone agreeing  it means that there is a a basically a  program  pre-established we all agree that this  is good and this is bad  and so that the dialogue so-called is  all about how do we get everyone to  agree upon  something which is the predetermined  agenda  no freedom let kids express themselves  let them disagree let them challenge so  the idea that kids would do this in the  classroom and that the teachers would  not try to impose  a set of values that they all have to  agree to  is a great idea so a great podcast  i if you understand portuguese i suggest  you listen to it  thank you for listening

